The control leaves **nothing** on disk, which is issue #5 reproduced rather than argued: 10 of
its 14 governed writes were gated and a reader of the repository cannot see one of them.

**(iii) batching — the pre-registered harm, and it is partly live.** q47's five lines sit in two
groups, at log lines 4–5 and 38–40, under a heading the arm invented, `## Write Gate records
(rules §3)`. The first pair precedes the work; the second group of three sits at line 38 of a
50-line log, after the step entries describing the code changes. So three of five were written
**after** the writes they gate, which is the shape the gate's own *"proceed only after step 2"*
denies. Not scored as a verdict on one arm; recorded, and read again across the other two B arms.

Worth noting against the obligation's own claim: q47's on-disk record names **5 of 12** governed
files while its conversation named **9 of 12**. The trace exists and is **a subset of the gate**,
so a reader auditing the repo would under-count the arm's own compliance.

